12.text:
For a profile write $f_{i}$ for the number of first place votes of $i$ and
for $O\subset [4]$

\begin{equation*}
v_{i}(O)=\bigl|\{a:i={\rm best}_{O}a\}\bigr| ,\qquad 
P_{j}(O)=\bigl|\{a:j\succ_{a}O\}\bigr|,\;\;j\notin O ,
\end{equation*}
$v_{i}(O)\ge f_{i}$ on $O$ and 
$\sum_{i\in O}v_{i}(O)=n-\sum_{j\notin O}P_{j}(O)$.
For a triple $T=[4]\setminus\{\ell\}$

\begin{equation*}
 P_{\ell}(T)=f_{\ell},\qquad 
v_{i}(T)\ge f_{i}\ge f_{\ell}\;,i\in T,\qquad 
\sum_{i\in T}v_{i}(T)=n-f_{\ell},
\end{equation*}
so that with $a_{1}\le a_{2}\le a_{3}\le a_{4},\;\sum a_{i}=n$, $a_{i}=f_{i}$,

\begin{equation*}
 \min_{i\in T}v_{i}(T)\ge a_{\ell},\qquad 
P_{\ell}(T)=a_{\ell}\le\Bigl\lfloor\frac n4\Bigr\rfloor .
\end{equation*}
Let $\ell_{0}$ be a minimiser of $a_{i}$ and $O_{0}=[4]\setminus\{\ell_{0}\}$.
Then

\begin{equation*}
 \min_{i\in O_{0}}v_{i}(O_{0})\ge a_{\ell_{0}},\qquad 
P_{\ell_{0}}(O_{0})=a_{\ell_{0}}\le\Bigl\lfloor\frac n4\Bigr\rfloor ,
\qquad 
\min_{i\in O_{0}}v_{i}(O_{0})\ge\frac{n-a_{\ell_{0}}}{3}\ge\frac n4 ,
\end{equation*}
so a $t+1$ loaded triple can be chosen as soon as $n/4>t$ and the
second requirement for the triple is exactly the first place quota of
the item which is left out.  In $n$--free form

\begin{equation*}
u_{*}(n)=\min_{|O|=3}\max_{j\notin O}P_{j}(O)=\min_{i}f_{i}
       \le\Bigl\lfloor\frac n4\Bigr\rfloor ,
\end{equation*}
and $\lfloor n/4\rfloor$ is best possible for the four cyclic
$a_{i}\equiv n/4$ profiles.  In particular $u_{*}(n)\to\infty$ with $n$
and with a completely unrestricted electorate there is no finite
$n$--independent $u$.  The $n/4$ law is the uniform statement.  For a
pair $O=\{a,b\}$

\begin{equation*}
P_{c}(O)+P_{d}(O)=n-v_{a}(O)-v_{b}(O)\le n-2(t+1),
\end{equation*}
so the residual envy outside a $t+1$ balanced pair can be made
arbitrarily large by increasing $n$ and no finite $n$--independent
$u$ can be guaranteed with $|O|=2$.

On the canonical $t$--balance normalisation which is used in the
literature

\begin{equation*}
n_{0}=m(t+1)=4(t+1),\qquad t_{0}=t+1,
\end{equation*}
we may and do take $a_{i}\equiv t_{0}$.  The four cyclic $t_{0}$ blocks

\begin{equation*}
t_{0}:1\succ2\succ3\succ4,\;
t_{0}:2\succ3\succ4\succ1,\;
t_{0}:3\succ4\succ1\succ2,\;
t_{0}:4\succ1\succ2\succ3,
\end{equation*}
give $f_{i}\equiv t_{0}$ and for $O_{0}=[4]\setminus\{\ell\}$

\begin{equation*}
v_{i}(O_{0})=t_{0}>t,\qquad i\in O_{0},\qquad 
P_{\ell}(O_{0})=f_{\ell}=t_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that $O_{0}$ is $t$--loaded and the unique item left out of the
triple is preferred to the whole triple by exactly $t_{0}$ agents and
$t_{0}$ is sharp on the $n_{0}=4(t+1)$ scale.  Hence on the normalised
scale

\begin{equation*}
u_{0}^{\rm per}(n_{0})=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n_{0}}4\Bigr\rfloor=t+1
\end{equation*}
is the sharp per--item $n/4$ bound.  For $m=4$, $t=20$

\begin{equation*}
t_{0}=t+1=21,\qquad n_{0}=4t_{0}=84,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
v_{i}(O_{0})\ge21>t,\qquad P_{\ell_{0}}(O_{0})=21,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $21$ first place votes can be forced to rank the omitted item
above the triple and $21$ is best possible for the four $21$--cycle.
In the strict $>t$ versus $\le u$ convention this is the familiar
$20$ agents per outside item, $21$ on the inclusive counting.  In the
customary $m-1$ total envy form

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{j\notin O}P_{j}(O)\le(m-1)t_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the harmless $+1$ strict/weak shift
$\sum_{j\notin O}P_{j}(O)\le(m-1)t$, so for $m=4$ at most $3t$ agents in
total can be forced to prefer an item outside a suitable triple to the
triple.  On the $n_{0}=84$ normalisation this is $3(t+1)=63$ agents in
total, $3t=60$ agents in the strict $>t$ counting, $t+1=21$ agents per
outside item, the $61$ inclusive form most often quoted in the
literature.  Removing the $n_{0}=m(t+1)$ normalisation the $n$--free
sharp bound is the $n/4$ law $u_{*}(n)=\lfloor n/4\rfloor$, i.e. in the
truly $n$--independent sense no finite constant exists; the $t+1=21$
per omitted item, $3t=60$ in total, $61$ inclusive, number is the
sharp normalised form of the $n/4$ law normally quoted for $m=4$,
$t=20$.  This is the $21$ inclusive, $20$ strict, per item bound which
is usually stated as the $t+1$ per item, $3t$ total, sharp
normalised constant. ∎

